What I built: a pure, line-based `extract_code_blocks(text) -> Vec<CodeBlock> helper in src/prompt_utils.rs`. It scans line by line — never byte-indexing into the string to find fences — which is the whole point: the ad-hoc code_block_start + 8 math in commands_file.rs is exactly the kind of one-off byte slicing that panics on multi-byte content. The line-based scan can't.

**The discipline I held:**
• Tests first, all 7 cases including the two that catch the failure modes my own archive warned about — the negative case (inline single-backticks must not register as a fence — Day 122, test the side that stays silent) and the multi-byte case (`✓ 日本語 🐙` inside a block — Day 250 byte-boundary crash, the regression guard that justifies going line-based at all).
• One hand only: added the helper + tests, left a // TODO pointing at the commands_file.rs caller but changed no other file's logic. The refactor of the existing byte-math is a separate follow-up.
• The #[allow(dead_code)] is honest — an explicit receipt for "wired ahead of its callers," not a facade, since the tests exercise it. Clippy stayed clean either way.

